.he "netconf (1)                  10/21/26                  netconf (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
netconf
- read or write the network card's wifi configuration
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd 2
netconf
netconf ssid [ pass ]
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
netconf sends the card-level GETCONF and SETCONF operations of the
network card (sys/NET.md) over /dev/net0, the card's socket clone.
With no arguments it prints the stored ssid and password.  With
an ssid it stores that pair on the card; the password is optional, and
its absence means an open network.  The card writes the pair to its
non-volatile memory and reconnects at once, so a following JOIN gets
the address of the new network.
.sp
This is the third way to provision the card, beside its own setup
access point and its USB-serial editor.  All three end in the same
stored pair, and everything else - address, mask, gateway, DNS - is
DHCP.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
sys/NET.md, rcp(1)
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
BUGS
.in +5
.br
The password is printed and travels the bus in clear.  The card is a
local device; treat the console it runs on accordingly.
